 "method": "Formula: prime-age employment rate = 100 x (sum over men and women, ages 25-29, 30-34, 35-44 and 45-54, of [civilian employed + in Armed Forces]) / (sum over the same eight sex-and-age groups of total population). The denominator is everyone in those age groups, including people in group quarters such as jails and nursing homes and people not in the labor force. Margins of error: sums use the root-sum-of-squares of the published cell margins, and the share uses the Census Bureau's formula for proportions. Citywide figures are for New York city (place 3651000); peers are matched on place GEOID (state + place FIPS) from data/peers.json; borough figures are the five counties.",
 "caveats": [
  "Armed Forces members count as employed; in New York City they are a tiny share of 25- to 54-year-olds.",
  "ACS employment status refers to the week before the survey, spread across the whole year; it is not the same concept or source as the BLS household-survey employment-population ratio.",
  "The ACS 1-year 2020 standard tables were never released (the Census Bureau published only experimental estimates because of pandemic data-collection problems), so 2020 is missing from every ACS series.",
  "The 2005 ACS covered only people in households; group quarters (dorms, prisons, nursing homes, shelters) were added in 2006. 2005 values are not strictly comparable to later years.",
  "All ACS figures are survey estimates. moe is the 90 percent margin of error, published by the Census Bureau for single cells and derived with the Bureau's approximation formulas for sums, shares and ratios."
 ]
}
